    "q-132993": {
      "dom_id": "question-132993",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=2x-6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-6)+k \\\\  & =x-3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "",
